[{"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","is_bot":false,"login":"rrnewton","name":"Ryan Newton"},"baseRefName":"main","baseRefOid":"0771253923fac12de6b1e1352227318548165c70","body":"[hermit2, dev-hermit, gpt-6-astra, devbig014, role=impl]\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nA DBT child must receive its parent-allocated virtual identity and complete its Tool thread-start hook before executing guest instructions. Track pending clones per virtual process, wait for authoritative parent publication, and retain an entry check in every basic block, including blocks compiled before the child existed. Failed clones release their count; a different process's exit cannot strand surviving thread startup. Copied native runtimes retain identity handling without invoking an inherited Rust Tool, and external global runtimes retain their supported Tool registration path.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe entry check reads the current thread's pending state from raw TLS. Once admitted, the thread skips the context call. The pending path preserves application registers and flags, including permitted Tool register changes; existing instrumenter reservations select the original unconditional context call. Returned clone-publication retries now fail after 60 seconds with a diagnostic naming virtual PID, host TID, pending clones and pending admission. A successful Tool admission is not rejected because the Tool legitimately blocked longer. The unused global pending counter is removed.\n\nActual context-writing calls exposed three bugs in the bundled DynamoRIO register manager: misplaced post-call saves could lose application RCX updates or corrupt RAX, and temporary allocations could reuse slots before restoring tool-owned values. The SDK correction keeps the updates after the call and preserves those pending restores. A permanent regression exercises the actual SDK across all general registers, XMM registers, selected flags, stack pointer and reserved tool registers.\n\nCurrent head is `51272804a0073c60d3dc73dbd396ba664f934bf9`, based on main `0771253923fac12de6b1e1352227318548165c70`. The three existing thread-admission commits were rebased with unchanged patches, authors and messages. SDK correction `8fad8ac5` and inline-check/deadline correction `51272804` are separate commits.\n\n## Determinism\n\nThe parent allocates the virtual child identity before clone. A new child waits for that identity and for the existing Tool thread-start hook to admit it before its first guest instruction. A host TID, callback arrival order or retry count does not select a successful guest-visible identity or scheduler turn. Every translated block retains the pending check, including blocks compiled before clone. The host monotonic deadline produces an explicit failure for returned retries; it does not choose a successful schedule or expire a Tool hook that blocks and then succeeds.\n\n## Linux Semantics\n\nHolding a child until its scheduler admission selects a permitted Linux parent/child execution order. Failed clone calls retain their actual error and release their pending count. CLONE_THREAD identity remains process-scoped, copied-process and vfork handling stay distinct, and guest register, flags and stack state must survive the admission call. The SDK fix preserves application context changes and tool-owned reservations. This change adds no signal-mask, restart, timer or syscall-result relaxation.\n\n## Validation\n\nCurrent source evidence, measured 2026-09-13:\n\n- The normal source-built SDK passed the permanent 9,612-case regression on nine threads. The identical fixture fails on the unchanged old SDK with a lost application RCX update. No assertion or reservation variant was removed.\n- The final normal production client build passed in 1.085 seconds; all seven explicitly selected thread-start tests passed in 1.136 seconds, including the new stalled-clone diagnostic and all six existing identity, clone, copied-process and precompiled-entry cases. The original per-fixture 10-second timeout and two-second forced cleanup remain.\n- The exact inline helper passed a separate 9,612-case native execution control. Both actual SDK restoration functions passed 84 supplied boundary contexts across original and cloned instruction lists. Removing the post-call RCX save caused concrete register corruption and failed the unchanged assertion with status 41; that negative traversal is incomplete, not a completed expected-status control. Supplied contexts are not captured application signals.\n- Five loop runs per variant, fifteen executions total, measured median whole-process times of 93.54 ms for this check, 110.84 ms for the unconditional call and 57.52 ms for a no-admission timing control. Every run retained the exact two-million-iteration result assertion. The no-admission variant will not ship. This narrower check reduces the measured cost but does not establish general performance or backend parity.\n\nThe old unconditional-call proposal did not satisfy the cost concern in https://github.com/rrnewton/reverie/pull/467#issuecomment-5652268048. This update implements its suggested inline alternative and addresses the retry diagnostic and unused-counter findings. The reviewer must assess resolution on this head; no owner acceptance of the earlier overhead is inferred.\n\nThe complete independent Codex review is approved at this head: https://github.com/rrnewton/reverie/pull/467#issuecomment-5656628793. Three fresh strict shared-Detcore-Tool verification pairs passed once at 2026-09-13 22:26:57–22:27:04 UTC. Each canonical BitwiseInfoV1 report retained full INFO and IO-buffer comparison, 102 equal records and 24,073 equal counted branches, with guest status 0. All six actual 22,505-byte INFO logs are retained and identical. The new native client was compiled from this head and loaded with the corrected SDK and the preserved real Detcore Tool. The Tool/CLI remain source-bound a2a/e471 builds with the documented relevant execution-path comparison to dade; this is not a whole-Hermit rebuild or universal backend qualification. The first preparation refused a host-/tmp fixture before guest execution; that failure remains retained. The successful preparation used the same binary at its original path outside /tmp.\n\nScoped Clippy and workspace formatting passed. The required separate Claude review remains outstanding, and the by-hand core-review protocol lint still lacks that approval. Keep this draft until the existing review requirements are met. No test, comparison, deadline or admission requirement was relaxed. The permanent SDK regression runs normally; the seven native thread-start cases follow the existing explicitly invoked convention. Full source review and new runtime evidence: /tmp/astra-ready-467-full-review-v1/final-report.md and strict-preparation-v2/execution/.\n\nEarlier external Detcore strict-verification pairs remain evidence for their original `d77cf68d` candidate and Hermit `cda9b404` artifacts. They are not current-head qualification. Those runs lacked coordinator COMMIT records, tracked in https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/issues/2993. Earlier broader pthread/fork probes failed on the prior client and main; the static example fixture could not link on this host. Those failures remain retained, with no complete scheduling-trace or general DBT qualification claimed.\n\nCommands, UTC times, source/binary identities, raw failures and cleanup records are under `/tmp/astra-root-reverie-467-recovery-20260913/`, especially `sdk-product-integration-v1`, `candidate-product-integration-v1`, `sdk-admission-execution-v8`, `sdk-restoration-control-v2` and `admission-performance-v3`, and verified notes on `astra-lane-drains-hermit-reverie-pr-queues-to-zero`. Current native source SHA256 is `7047b7a30fee1e1d415ab2da616df32aaf9ce264182b5329daf2d445b88e29ce`.\n\n## Human Review Required\n\nThe existing `post-facto-human-review` requirement applies under trigger 2: the Reverie runtime thread-identity interface and Tool thread-start admission are core abstractions. This requires the separate approving review lanes and the by-hand core-review protocol lint. A source-only report or a bounded helper review does not stand in for the remaining full approval. No new approval trigger or owner-only review requirement is introduced.\n\nPrior public candidates remain preserved at `archive/astra-reverie-pr467-reviewed-d77cf68d` and `archive/astra-reverie-pr467-reviewed-d317ff25`; the original nine-file proposal remains at `archive/astra-reverie-pr467-original-d2216e8d`. The remaining Tool/host identity API, strict namespace and injected-result work is tracked separately in https://github.com/rrnewton/reverie/issues/547.\n","headRefName":"salvage/scorecard-dirty-20260822","headRefOid":"51272804a0073c60d3dc73dbd396ba664f934bf9","isDraft":true,"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOTfjBAs8AAAACs6G19w","name":"post-facto-human-review","description":"Landed after autonomous adversarial review; human follow-up requested","color":"D4C5F9"},{"id":"LA_kwDOTfjBAs8AAAACuvsSrw","name":"passed-review-codex","description":"Codex adversarial review passed at the current PR head","color":"0E8A16"},{"id":"LA_kwDOTfjBAs8AAAACvMfgcA","name":"adversarial-review-codex1","description":"Codex adversarial review round 1 completed","color":"5319e7"}],"number":467,"state":"OPEN","title":"Admit DBT threads before executing guest instructions","updatedAt":"2026-09-14T20:42:58Z","url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/reverie/pull/467"}]
